WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
In this video, you'll add the Fedex Shipping method to a WooCommerce shipping zone, so your customers see live FedEx prices at checkout.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:15.114
FedEx prices appear at checkout only when the plugin's method sits in a shipping zone that covers the customer's address.

00:00:15.564 --> 00:00:22.092
In your WordPress admin, go to WooCommerce, Settings, Shipping. This page lists your shipping zones.

00:00:22.542 --> 00:00:30.558
A new store has one built-in row — Locations not covered by your other zones — which matches any address no other zone covers.

00:00:31.008 --> 00:00:38.304
Open the zone — click Edit next to it. This demo uses a throwaway zone — you'll use the zone that covers your customers.

00:00:38.754 --> 00:00:46.098
Add the Fedex Shipping method to the zone that covers your customers: click Add shipping method, choose Fedex Shipping, and confirm.

00:00:46.548 --> 00:00:54.660
The spelling is exact — lowercase x. Confirm with Continue, then save the window — and the method joins the zone's list.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:01:05.238
Its Edit window has only two generic fields — on or off, and a title. There are no FedEx settings here; every option lives on the plugin's own Settings page.

00:01:05.688 --> 00:01:13.224
The toggle in the method list turns FedEx prices on or off for that zone. Add the method to every zone you deliver to.

00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:17.946
That's the whole setup. Next: see the rates customers get at checkout.

